// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
//
//! The build half of the runtime route: three modules and one generated file.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links the native library. That is the point: in this
//! configuration the symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time, and
//! linking the archive as well would make the linker demand them before `load`
//! ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core, supplied by the driver.
//!             Pure Zig; imported by the facade in both configurations, because
//!             `LoadSource` and `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must
//!             be the same types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding and the one load-time check.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output: the table, the wrappers, `load`, and the re-exported
//!             translated namespace. It imports `rt`, `library` and the
//!             generator's patched translation as `c_native`, which imports the
//!             dispatch back as `c_routed`.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=luajit_rt`, so
//!             its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this dispatch.
//! The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the graph should
//! reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols statically and the
//! runtime route silently becomes a linked one.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds.
/// The two must agree, and a build where they do not fails to link, which is
/// the whole error report: there is no runtime path that could hide it.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance.
const sync_prefix = "luajit_rt";

/// `extern` declarations that the C library provides and that must therefore
/// stay direct.
///
/// ## Why this list is as long as it is
///
/// LuaJIT's four public headers include exactly one C library header between
/// them, and it is not optional: `src/lauxlib.h:13` includes `<stdio.h>` for
/// `BUFSIZ`, which `src/luaconf.h:113` turns into `LUAL_BUFFERSIZE`, which is
/// the length of the array inside `luaL_Buffer` (`src/lauxlib.h:135-140`).
/// So every function `<stdio.h>` declares is an `extern` declaration in this
/// build's translation, exactly like `lua_pushinteger` is, and the generator
/// cannot tell the two apart: it sees one list of `pub extern` declarations
/// and routes everything it is not told to leave alone.
///
/// ## How the list was derived
///
/// Not by reading headers and not from a list of what a C library "usually"
/// declares: by running the pinned compiler's own `zig translate-c` over this
/// package's aggregate translation unit, `build_support/translate.h`, once for
/// every target, and keeping every `pub extern fn|var|const` name that is not
/// LuaJIT's own namespace. The targets are the published 53-platform Zig
/// 0.16.0 Tier 1/2 table plus this package's six `check` targets
/// (`build.zig:163-170`), and each one is read through the front end
/// `build_support/graph.zig:768 routeFor` gives it -- Aro directly on a GNU
/// libc target, and `zig cc -E -dD -fno-blocks` in front of it everywhere
/// else, because Aro's copy of the C library cannot parse those targets'
/// headers. All 59 targets translate, and the list below is the union of their
/// extern names minus the 149 that `src/*.h` declares, so it is a superset of
/// what the generator can be asked about for any of them.
///
/// That measurement is what put the C libraries the previous list did not
/// cover into it: the mingw secure-append block and `corecrt_stdio_config.h`
/// (`any-windows-any/sec_api/stdio_s.h`, `any-windows-any/stdio.h`), the
/// Darwin `_stdio.h` and `secure/_stdio.h` variants
/// (`any-darwin-any/sys/stdio.h`), the FreeBSD `ssp/stdio.h` and
/// `xlocale/_stdio.h` spellings, the NetBSD and OpenBSD `<stdio.h>` entries,
/// the 45 `wasm-wasi-musl/wasi/api.h` entry points that WASI's `<stdio.h>`
/// reaches, and the three stream objects `stdin`, `stdout` and `stderr` that
/// every one of these libcs declares.
///
/// Their union is listed, not any one libc's, because the list is compiled
/// into the build file and the build file is target-independent. A name a
/// given target's `<stdio.h>` does not declare simply matches nothing, which
/// is harmless; a libc name left out of this list would be demanded from the
/// provider at load time and refused, which is not.
///
/// Names that are macros in one libc and functions in another (`getc`, `putc`,
/// `fdopen`), and the redirect aliases glibc installs for the C99 scan family,
/// are both listed. `stdio.h` is the only C library header these four headers
/// reach, so this list is complete for them; `assert.h` is the next candidate
/// and is unreachable, because `src/luaconf.h:144-145` only includes it when
/// `LUA_USE_ASSERT` or `LUA_USE_APICHECK` is defined, and neither is.
///
/// ### The fortified and alias declarations an optimized translation adds
///
/// The union above was measured over the aggregate as `translate-c` sees it,
/// but `translate-c` reads the target's `<stdio.h>` with the *build's*
/// configuration, and the `-Doptimize=ReleaseSafe` translations turn
/// `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` on where the Debug, ReleaseFast and ReleaseSmall ones do
/// not:
/// `generic-glibc/features.h:454-473` defines `__USE_FORTIFY_LEVEL` only when
/// `_FORTIFY_SOURCE > 0` **and** `__OPTIMIZE__ > 0`, and
/// `generic-glibc/stdio.h:956-959` is what includes `bits/stdio2-decl.h` behind
/// it. That header declares a second family of externs an unfortified pass
/// never sees: a `__*_chk` implementation per fortified function, a `__*_alias`
/// redirect back onto the plain function, and a `__*_chk_warn` sibling. The C
/// library declares them and the C library exports them; `libluajit-5.1`
/// exports none of them, and a provider that happened to pull libc in would
/// satisfy them only by accident, through `dlsym` searching the provider's
/// dependencies.
///
/// That is not an argument, it is what a failing configuration's own generated
/// dispatch shows: the ReleaseSafe table required **163** names where every
/// other configuration's table required **148**, and the fifteen names the two
/// differ by are exactly fifteen of the names below. Both loaded ReleaseSafe
/// gates were therefore refused with `MissingSymbol` against a provider that
/// exports all 148 of LuaJIT's entry points and no libc name at all, while the
/// same provider loaded under the other modes.
///
/// Twenty-three names are added, each read out of the pinned Zig 0.16.0
/// distribution's `lib/libc/include/generic-glibc/bits/stdio2-decl.h`:
///
///   * `__fprintf_chk` (`:49`), `__printf_chk` (`:52`), `__vfprintf_chk`
///     (`:53`) and `__vprintf_chk` (`:56`), under `#if __USE_FORTIFY_LEVEL > 1`
///     (`:47`);
///   * `__dprintf_chk` (`:60`) and `__vdprintf_chk` (`:62`), under
///     `# ifdef __USE_XOPEN2K8` (`:59`);
///   * `__asprintf_chk` (`:69`), `__vasprintf_chk` (`:72`),
///     `__obstack_printf_chk` (`:75`) and `__obstack_vprintf_chk` (`:79`), under
///     `# ifdef __USE_GNU` (`:67`);
///   * `__gets_warn` (`:89`), the `__REDIRECT` sibling of the `__gets_chk` this
///     list already named (`:93`);
///   * `__fgets_alias` (`:96`), `__fgets_chk_warn` (`:100`) and `__fgets_chk`
///     (`:102`);
///   * `__fread_alias` (`:110`), `__fread_chk_warn` (`:114`) and `__fread_chk`
///     (`:118`);
///   * `__fgets_unlocked_alias` (`:127`), `__fgets_unlocked_chk_warn` (`:131`)
///     and `__fgets_unlocked_chk` (`:133`), under `#ifdef __USE_GNU` (`:126`);
///   * `__fread_unlocked_alias` (`:145`), `__fread_unlocked_chk_warn` (`:149`)
///     and `__fread_unlocked_chk` (`:153`), under `#ifdef __USE_MISC` (`:143`).
///
/// Eight of the twenty-three sit behind a guard this pin's translation does
/// not turn on -- the four `__USE_GNU` names, the three `__fgets_unlocked_*`
/// names and `__gets_warn` -- and are listed for the same reason the rest of
/// this list is a union: a name a given target's `<stdio.h>` does not declare
/// simply matches nothing, which is harmless.
///
/// None of the twenty-three is a `lua`, `luaL_`, `luaJIT_` or `luaopen_` name
/// and none is declared by `lua.h`, `lauxlib.h`, `lualib.h` or the generated
/// `luajit.h`, so nothing native-owned is de-routed by listing them, and the
/// 149 names `src/*.h` declares stay 149 in every optimize mode. They are
/// listed by exact name rather than by a `__` prefix rule: a prefix rule would
/// also swallow a legitimate name this file cannot see, and the generator's
/// lookup is by exact name anyway.
const libc_noise =
    \\_LIBC_COUNT,_LIBC_SINGLE_BY_DEFAULT,__MINGW_GNU_PRINTF,
    \\__MINGW_GNU_SCANF,__MINGW_MS_PRINTF,__MINGW_MS_SCANF,__REDIRECT,
    \\__REDIRECT_NTH,__acrt_iob_func,__asprintf,__builtin_va_end,
    \\__builtin_va_start,__deprecated_msg,__fbufsize,__flbf,__fpending,
